%% The Topic 4 key diagrams, redrawn simply — RN volume only (included by RN.gsm).

## The key diagrams

Four diagrams carry most of Topic 4. Learn to draw each one from memory in **under two minutes**. A diagram placed early in an answer shows the examiner the whole argument at a glance and earns credit in its own right.

::: fig flow | From development theories to gender approaches.
@per 3
Modernisation: women left out
WID (1970s): add women
Dependency and Marxism: exploitation
WAD (late 1970s): women in, unequally
Feminist theory of power
GAD (1980s): change gender relations
:::

**Draw it in:** WID, WAD and GAD (2016, 2021, 2025). It shows that each approach answered the failure of the one before.

::: fig compare | Modernisation and dependency on women.
## Modernisation
- Cause: tradition inside
- Cure: growth, technology
- Women: freed by modernity
- Misses: structures, colonialism
## Dependency
- Cause: exploitation from outside
- Cure: self-reliance
- Women: ignored
- Misses: local patriarchy, unpaid work
:::

**Draw it in:** modernisation, dependency and subordination (2019), and the development-theory questions of 2021, 2024 and 2025.

::: fig pyramid | From WID to GAD — how the approaches deepened.
WID: add women to development
WAD: see how development exploits women
GAD: change power between women and men
:::

**Draw it in:** every WID/WAD/GAD question, including the 2017 note. Read it bottom-up as "access → structure → power".

::: fig cycle | Moser's triple role of women.
@centre The triple role
Productive: farm, wage and home-based work
Reproductive: childcare, cooking, caring
Community-managing: unpaid water, health and school work
:::

**Draw it in:** the note on practical and strategic needs (2018), and any GAD or globalisation answer that needs "women's work is undercounted".
